<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lazarica Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lazarica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lazarica Church is a medieval Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a>, built in the 1370s or 1380s as the court church of Prince Lazar. Dedicated to Saint Stephen, it is one of the finest examples of the Morava school of architecture, with a trefoil plan and richly carved stone decoration on its façades. It stands in the remains of Lazar's fortress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lazarica-church.jpg" alt="Lazarica Church" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sun82 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lazarica_Church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its full name is the Church of the Holy First Martyr Stephen. It was an endowment of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar of Serbia</a>, who built it at the same time as the fortifications of his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a>, as the palace church of the Kruševac Fortress. The main account of its founding is in the life of Despot Stefan Lazarević written by Constantine of Kostenets. Of the large fortress complex, only the church and parts of the keep remain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Lazarica is regarded as the prototype of the Morava school of architecture. Its plan is a trefoil, a variant of the cruciform plan, three bays long, with a dome over the central space and the narthex, which originally had open side passages. The apse is semicircular inside and five-sided outside, with attached colonnettes, and the building is turned five degrees from a true west to east line. Inside, the main dome rises 17.25 metres, and the walls are between 1 and 1.75 metres thick.</p><p>The masonry is essentially Byzantine in style: continuous horizontal courses of dressed white sandstone alternate with three rows of brick set in thick plaster, without randomly placed bricks. A particular technique was used to draw the thick mortar joints out from the wall face, giving the façades their banded look.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1455. The church was abandoned and desecrated, used as a stable for horses, and its roof was taken down for use elsewhere. During the Russo-Austrian-Turkish War of 1736 to 1739 it was partly rebuilt and its interior painted with frescoes by Andra Andrejević, before the town returned to Ottoman rule. The first major reconstruction followed the founding of the independent Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, with many alterations over the next hundred years. In 1979 Lazarica was declared a Monument of Culture of Exceptional Importance, protected by the Republic of Serbia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lazarica Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Churches such as Ravanica and <b>Lazarica</b> gave their name to the Morava school of architecture.</blockquote><a href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
